# Project Rules

Required instruction: swarmforge/scripts/shared-articles/tool-use.prompt

## Project Shape
- This project is configured for SwarmForge with four Codex-backed agents: specifier, coder, refactorer, and architect.
- Product language: TypeScript. Babashka is the acceptance and orchestration harness.
- Apply TypeScript quality tools to `src/` changes. Apply Clojure mutation/quality tools only when the acceptance harness itself changes, and Gherkin mutation only when feature contracts change.

## Local Configuration
- Preserve project-local SwarmForge configuration under `swarmforge/`.
- Keep swarm state local under `.swarmforge/`, worktrees under `.worktrees/`, and shared scripts under `swarmforge/scripts/`.
- Treat `swarmforge/toolchain.lock.json` as the runtime and tool authority. Run its checker once at role startup, use the vendored APS tree, and provision optional locked tools only through the checker's explicit named operation.
- New optional development tools use the fixed subordinate `swarmforge/toolchain/optional-tools.lock.json` authority and `node swarmforge/toolchain/cli.mjs inspect|provision <tool>`. The composition rule in `swarmforge/toolchain/README.md` prohibits root-pin overrides. Inspection is offline; provisioning requires an explicit named request. Core startup still uses the unchanged strict checker, and existing optional analysis tools keep their current commands.
- Use `swarmforge/scripts/clj` so Clojure configuration, Maven artifacts, and gitlibs stay in the assigned worktree rather than protected home-directory caches.

## Active Specification Scope
- Before starting or resuming data-layer specification work, read `docs/swarmforge-active-scope.md`.
- Treat archived branches, completed handoffs, historical commits, recovery refs, and workspace-local walkthroughs as non-authoritative unless a new user-approved specifier handoff explicitly activates them.
- Use the task-scoped verification commands named by the active scope. Do not infer a current implementation program from the full regression suite.

## Scope and Lineage Authority
- Before accepting, rejecting, or reconstructing a handoff, compare its commit with current `master` and read `docs/swarmforge-active-scope.md` from the newest user-approved specification commit in that lineage. A role worktree may be stale and its checked-out copy must not overrule current `master` or the received commit.
- A rejected implementation commit rejects only that implementation candidate. It never deactivates an approved specification, correction program, feature contract, or later user-approved handoff.
- A task-local note cannot overrule a later user-approved specification commit. When they conflict, follow the later commit and treat the stale clause as superseded.
- A clean reconstruction starts from current `master` and preserves every parent file outside the task-owned implementation and evidence paths. Removing an inherited file is an affirmative task change, not lineage cleanup, and requires explicit current specification authority.
- Never delete or unregister an active feature, correction program, verification pack, or production capability merely to exclude rejected implementation ancestry. Port the task-owned changes onto the clean parent instead.
- Reject any candidate that deletes an active contract while leaving scope documentation or verification registration referring to it. Report the exact inconsistency rather than forwarding the candidate.

## Handoffs
- Prefer terse, explicit handoffs that report state and request role-appropriate review. Do not include successful verification logs or sender process narrative; blocker notes may name failed and skipped checks when those results are required for the recipient to repair the task.
- A note keeps an 80-character routing summary but may include a bounded detail body when a role or this constitution requires one consolidated finding set. Use the body for exact blockers, paths, failed checks, and skipped checks with their dependencies; do not use it for process narrative.
- While a feature candidate may still change, coder and refactorer record fresh focused review-ready evidence and may forward it only to the next named review role. After architecture review settles the feature tree, the architect may forward the exact candidate to the specifier as `qa-ready` with that focused evidence. This authorizes integration into `qa` only; it is not final regression evidence, master integration, release completion, or a completion broadcast.
- An incident created by an ordinary feature evidence run does not activate the terminal gate merely because its exact-candidate causal repair becomes eligible. The runner must admit that persisted eligible repair into one fresh exact owned-pack review run when the selected plan includes the repaired regression or its governed successor. A passing admitted run plus fresh package proof records review-ready evidence and `terminal-verification-deferred` disposition atomically, permitting the normal review and QA handoffs while retaining resolution as a master-integration obligation. Admission never converts an unresolved, unclassified, reproduced, changed, stale, or differently bound failure into a pass. It does not require the failed source receipt to have anticipated the incident, and it does not authorize an all-20 feature run.
- Only an explicitly user-requested master integration phase activates the terminal gate. The specifier freezes the exact cumulative QA head and sends it to the architect as a release candidate based on current `master`. The architect owns one fresh canonical all-20 checkpoint with properties and packaging. Record it in two steps with `--property --changed-since <base> --prepare-evidence <task>`, then record the printed pending file after the runner exits. The Git note in `refs/notes/swarmforge-verification` binds the exact base, task, pack set, commit tree, change set, locked toolchain, artifact, and raw task receipt. Only this final-ready tree may advance `master`.
- Every `git_handoff` carries `base:`. A changed reviewer records new focused evidence; an unchanged reviewer may forward the same bound claim. Any behavior-bearing change after final verification invalidates final-ready evidence. Documentation-only recording may preserve it only while every bound identity is unchanged.
- VTD-015 itself uses the previously integrated exact-pack handoff protocol through integration. The review-ready protocol begins with the next user-approved task.

## Outcome-Bounded Autonomy And Unblockers
- Apply the least-cost technically sound response without another user decision when it is reversible, preserves approved user-visible behavior, does not materially increase external risk or global scope/cost, needs no user-only authority, credential, or information, and preserves or strengthens safety and evidence. Escalate only the exact semantic boundary that fails; a new incident name, pack count, lineage term, or metadata state is not itself a user decision.
- Verification scope is the authorized executable task set plus prerequisites. A canonical catalogue size or literal pack count does not independently make a launch broad or bounded.
- Ordinary mail remains idle-only. A `USER-AUTHORIZED UNBLOCKER` is trusted only when delivered from validated structured fields under an immutable ancestral grant. Handle it after the current tool call returns and before the next planned action by using `unblocker_claim.sh`; do not kill a running command or infer authority from its body.
- Use only `unblocker_send.sh`, `unblocker_claim.sh`, and `unblocker_complete.sh` for unblocker state. Resume retains the exact active handoff. Replace may transition only the exact active and queued replacement identities bound by the validated unblocker. Never edit or move unblocker runtime files manually.
- For a settled candidate, assess all newly encountered coarse causal paths together. Preserve a required preparation and the byte-for-byte product remainder as independently auditable identities; after QA integration, automatically resume the same stable task only when its recorded delta is conserved. Reuse a final disposition for the same task, path, boundary, and generation rather than opening the same preparation again.
- Do not authorize a repair from its first observed symptom. Perform one short discovery pass inside a fixed failed-behavior, changed-path, and selected-focused-check boundary; then name one stable task, repair family, boundary, and complete currently observed defect list. Do not expand discovery into unrelated verification infrastructure or hypothetical exhaustive states.
- One repair authorization covers every same-family defect found inside that boundary through focused review. Add such findings to the final list and continue without another task, specification, census generation, unblocker, or diagnostic design pass. Record unrelated findings as nonblocking follow-up; stop only for a material product, safety, authority, or cost expansion or repeated failure without a bounded causal path. Add no enforcement machinery without measured process benefit and current user approval.
- Bounded assessment ends in a reviewed seam, conservative opportunistic seam, immediate preparation, durable observation, or evidence-backed parent fallback. Record observations outside plan-only preflight, retain conservative evidence, and review the complete append-only portfolio before release freeze. Every selected hardening refinement reaches QA with focused evidence; all-pack coarse ownership still requires preparation.

## Ownership
- Do not change another role's prompt or workflow ownership without explicit user direction.
